        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "68f7068e-1333-54d4-9533-0f675ddd25ec",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Organizations: 93rd United States Congress\n- People: Richard Nixon, Gerald Ford, Spiro Agnew\n- Places: Washington, DC\n- Concepts: Vice President of the United States, 25th Amendment\n\nFacts:\n- Spiro Agnew served as the 39th Vice President of the United States from 1969 to 1973.\n- The 93rd United States Congress met in Washington, DC from January 3, 1973, to January 3, 1975.\n- The 93rd United States Congress took place during the end of Richard Nixon's presidency and the beginning of Gerald Ford's presidency.\n- After Spiro Agnew resigned, Gerald Ford was appointed under the authority of the newly ratified 25th Amendment.\n- The 93rd United States Congress was the first and, to date, only Congress to have more than two Senate Presidents, with three Vice Presidents of the United States serving in that role.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "5f0d613f-e473-5b91-a205-c0d83278702a",
        "text": "Page: 93rd United States Congress. Sentence: It met in Washington, DC from January 3, 1973, to January 3, 1975, during the end of Richard Nixon's presidency, and the beginning of Gerald Ford's.\nPage: 93rd United States Congress. Sentence: This Congress was the first (and, to date, only) Congress with more than two Senate Presidents (the Vice President of the United States), in this case, three.\nPage: 93rd United States Congress. Sentence: After the resignation of Spiro Agnew, Gerald Ford was appointed under the authority of the newly ratified 25th Amendment.\nPage: Spiro Agnew. Sentence: Spiro Theodore \"Ted\" Agnew ( ; November 9, 1918 – September 17, 1996) was the 39th Vice President of the United States, serving from 1969 to 1973.",
